 "standard_text": "Continuity and differentiability, chain rule, derivative of inverse trigonometric functions, like sin⁻¹ x, cos⁻¹ x and tan⁻¹ x, derivative of implicit functions; concept of exponential and logarithmic functions; derivatives of logarithmic and exponential functions; logarithmic differentiation, derivative of functions expressed in parametric forms; second order derivatives (summary of the CBSE syllabus)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 12 Continuity and Differentiability worksheet (NCERT Chapter 5): continuity of piecewise functions, chain rule, inverse trigonometric, exponential and log derivatives, implicit, parametric and logarithmic differentiation and second derivatives. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Chain rule: dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx).",
 "key_sub": "f is continuous at a when the left limit, the right limit and f(a) are equal.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>f is <b>continuous</b> at c if lim<sub>x→c</sub> f(x) = f(c): both one-sided limits must equal f(c).</li><li><b>Chain rule</b>: d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x)) g′(x). Example: d/dx sin(3x) = 3 cos 3x.</li><li>d/dx(e<sup>x</sup>) = e<sup>x</sup>, d/dx(log x) = 1/x, d/dx(sin<sup>−1</sup> x) = 1/√(1 − x²), d/dx(tan<sup>−1</sup> x) = 1/(1 + x²).</li><li>Implicit: differentiate both sides, treating <i>y</i> as a function of x. Parametric: dy/dx = (dy/dt) ÷ (dx/dt).</li><li>For <i>y</i> = f(x)<sup>g(x)</sup>, take logs first (logarithmic differentiation).</li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find dy/dx if y = sin(x²).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Outer: cos(x²); inner: x² with derivative 2x</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2x cos(x²)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Forgetting to multiply by the derivative of the inside.</li><li>Using (dx/dt) ÷ (dy/dt) for a parametric curve.</li><li>Writing d/dx(xˣ) = x · xˣ⁻¹.</li></ul>",
